-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTALL
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Requirements
------------
 - git
 - a C/C++ compiler (gcc/g++ or clang)
 - cmake >= 3.10
 - pkg-config
 - Qt 6 (Core, Gui, Widgets, Svg); Qt 5 is used as a fallback if Qt 6 is not
   found. On Windows, Qt 6 additionally requires Core5Compat.
 - libglib >= 2.32.0
 - zlib
 - libusb-1.0 >= 1.0.16
	On FreeBSD and OpenBSD this is part of the base system.
 - libboost >= 1.42 (headers only)
 - libfftw3 >= 3.3
 - Python 3 development files (headers and libpython)

The Qt Svg module is not optional, and CMake stops if it is missing. The themes
draw all checkboxes, combobox arrows and window buttons from SVG files. If the
qsvg plugin is missing at runtime, those controls are not painted, and DSView
only logs a warning.


Step 1: Install the requirements
--------------------------------
Check your distribution's package manager if yours is not listed.

Debian/Ubuntu:
 $ sudo apt install build-essential cmake git pkg-config \
	qt6-base-dev libqt6svg6-dev libgl1-mesa-dev libxkbcommon-dev libvulkan-dev \
	libglib2.0-dev zlib1g-dev libusb-1.0-0-dev libboost-dev libfftw3-dev \
	python3-dev libudev-dev

   For a Qt 5 build, use qtbase5-dev and libqt5svg5-dev instead of the
   qt6/vulkan/xkbcommon/mesa packages.

Fedora:
 $ sudo dnf install gcc-c++ cmake git pkgconf qt6-qtbase-devel qt6-qtsvg-devel \
	glib2-devel zlib-devel libusb1-devel boost-devel fftw-devel \
	python3-devel systemd-devel

Arch:
 $ sudo pacman -S base-devel git cmake glib2 zlib libusb boost fftw python \
	qt6-base qt6-svg

macOS (Homebrew):
 $ brew install cmake pkg-config glib libusb boost fftw python qt

Windows: see "Building for Windows" below.


Step 2: Get the source code
---------------------------
 $ git clone https://github.com/mrehkopf/DSView.git
 $ cd DSView


Step 3: Build and install
-------------------------
 $ cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
 $ cmake --build build -j"$(nproc)"
 $ sudo cmake --install build

On Linux, installing places DSView in <prefix>/bin (default prefix: /usr/local),
and the firmware, demo files and translations in <prefix>/share/DSView. The
protocol decoders go to <prefix>/share/libsigrokdecode4DSL. It also installs a
desktop entry and the udev rule 60-dreamsourcelab.rules, which allows
non-root users to access the hardware. After the first installation, reload
the udev rules (or reconnect the device):
 $ sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger

To build a Debian package instead of installing directly:
 $ cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr
 $ cmake --build build -j"$(nproc)"
 $ cd build && cpack -G DEB

See .github/workflows/build.yml for how the CI builds the .deb and AppImage
packages, including bundling Python so they do not depend on the host's
Python version.


Building for Windows
--------------------
Native build with MSYS2 (this is what CI uses). In a UCRT64 shell:
 $ pacman -S git mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-{toolchain,cmake,ninja,qt6-base,qt6-svg,qt6-5compat,glib2,zlib,libusb,boost,fftw,python,pkg-config}
 $ cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
	-DPython3_ROOT_DIR=/ucrt64 -DPython3_FIND_STRATEGY=LOCATION \
	-DPython3_FIND_REGISTRY=NEVER
 $ cmake --build build

The Python3_* options make sure DSView links against MSYS2's Python rather
than another Python installed on the system. The "windows" job in
.github/workflows/build.yml shows how the runtime DLLs are bundled.

Cross-compiling from Linux with MXE:
 $ cd cross-compile-windows
 $ MXE_HOME=/path/to/mxe ./dsview-build-mxe.sh
   (or run with --auto-mxe to clone MXE into $MXE_HOME automatically)

This builds a static 64-bit Qt 6 DSView.exe in build.dir together with an
embedded Python distribution. Set QT_MAJOR=5 to build against Qt 5 instead,
which is required for a 32-bit build. MXE additionally needs Perl Time::Piece
and Python sphinx on the build host. The header of dsview-build-mxe.sh describes
incremental rebuilds, deployment and creating an installer with cpack.


The latest source code:
 https://github.com/mrehkopf/DSView
